    "seo": {
      "title": "Insurance Claims Systems in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iranian digital claims workflows, including online reporting, expert assessment, supporting documents, and the limits of automation."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "online-claims",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A digital entrance to a longer process",
        "content": "A claim begins when someone reports a loss and seeks compensation under an insurance policy. Moving that report online changes access to the process, but does not by itself establish that assessment and settlement are automatic.\n\nAzki, an Iranian insurance comparison and ordering platform, describes a service covering request registration, loss assessment, and online payment. Its claims page limits participation to eligible policies and participating insurers. For corporate supplementary health insurance, it describes collecting supporting documents and forwarding them to the insurer. This is a coordinated service involving document handling, rather than evidence that every stage occurs without human intervention. [s1]\n\nThe platform's homepage gives a more detailed motor-claims sequence: the user identifies the insurance type and their role in the accident, supplies insurer and incident information, and is contacted by an assessment expert. It describes payment following assessment and the injured party's approval. This establishes the advertised workflow, not independently verified payment speed or customer outcomes. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "physical-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why physical evidence can remain important",
        "content": "A Saman Insurance electronic-equipment policy document hosted by retailer 19kala illustrates another arrangement. Its claims instructions begin with an online form, followed by contact from an assessor. The subsequent steps include attending a designated branch with identification, the damaged device, its packaging and accessories, and the insurance certificate. Assessment and repair documentation then support the claim. [s3]\n\nFor repairable damage, the instructions request a formal repair invoice and replaced parts; for a total loss, they request a written technical assessment that the device cannot be repaired. The document also provides a branch-based alternative when online registration fails or internet access is unavailable. These are terms in a specific, undated document, not a statement of current requirements for every Saman policy. [s3]\n\nThe example shows why an online form should not be treated as proof of a completely remote claims service. The evidence needed to substantiate a damaged physical object can extend beyond the information entered on a screen. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "claims-workflow",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "The equipment-claims sequence described in the Saman document",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Policyholder\"] -->|\"Submits electronic claim form\"| B[\"Online notification\"]\n  B -->|\"Prompts assessor contact\"| C[\"Assessor\"]\n  C -->|\"Directs branch attendance\"| D[\"Branch receives device and documents\"]\n  D -->|\"Refers for technical assessment\"| E[\"Designated technical centre\"]\n  E -->|\"Provides repair or total-loss evidence\"| F[\"Supporting claim documents\"]\n  F -->|\"Submitted for settlement process\"| G[\"Settlement and payment\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The arrows describe procedural steps in the inspected equipment-policy document. They do not establish current service availability, automatic approval, or a universal Iranian claims process.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "measuring-service",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the available evidence cannot measure",
        "content": "These examples document service designs with different boundaries. They do not provide a comparable measure of how often claims finish remotely, how long completed cases take, or how frequently decisions are disputed. A defensible assessment of system performance would require outcome data alongside the advertised steps. The present evidence therefore supports an explanation of workflows, while leaving nationwide effectiveness unresolved. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
