    "description": "Online insurance sales bring product information, comparison, ordering, and issuance support into a digital customer journey. Azki's published descriptions illustrate this model in Iran while showing that online ordering can still involve official information checks and professional review.",
    "tags": ["Insurance", "Online sales", "Digital distribution", "Insurance platforms", "Iran"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T18:49:00Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T18:49:00Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Online Insurance Sales in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore how Iranian online insurance sales connect comparison, ordering, information checks, and issuance, with clear limits on the available evidence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "distribution-model",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "An online seller and an insurer have different roles",
        "content": "For an international reader, the first distinction is between the website used to arrange insurance and the company providing the policy. Azki explicitly states that it is not an insurance company. It presents its service as a way to compare insurers and purchase coverage, while its frequently asked questions describe a platform for insurance information, management, and ordering. [s1][s2]\n\nThis makes online sales a distribution subject as well as a software subject. The visible customer journey can combine information about products with assistance in completing an order. A comparison screen is one stage in that journey; policy issuance is another. Azki's descriptions retain a role for professional intermediaries and information checks rather than presenting every purchase as an unattended transaction. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "ordering-and-issuance",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What happens between an order and a policy",
        "content": "Azki says its staff check required information against official systems before issuance and contact customers when details are incorrect or inconsistent. It also advertises renewal reminders. These features address different stages of the relationship: establishing usable information for an order and helping an existing customer remember a future renewal. [s1]\n\nThe platform's frequently asked questions distinguish the ability to submit requests at any time from published staff response hours. This is a useful qualification to the phrase 'online insurance': continuous access to a form does not necessarily mean continuous human processing. Its FAQ also says that formal professional participants control the processes through which it works with insurers approved by Central Insurance of Iran. That is the platform's description of its arrangements, rather than an independently checked license record. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-service-claims",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How to interpret the available evidence",
        "content": "The inspected material establishes that a provider publicly describes an online comparison and ordering service with human checks. It does not establish that the platform includes every insurer, that every product can be issued immediately, or that online distribution produces lower total costs. Those conclusions would require evidence beyond the provider's explanation of its own service. [s1][s2]\n\nA fuller account of this branch of Iranian insurance technology also needs current regulatory documentation, verified licensing information, and evidence from multiple distribution channels. This page therefore treats Azki as a documented example rather than a proxy for the whole market. Its practical significance is the connection between a customer-facing digital interface and the professional work behind issuance; the scale and effectiveness of that connection remain research questions. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
